WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.710
- One of the tools you
will very frequently use

00:00:02.710 --> 00:00:05.320
in consulting and in many
other professional roles

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:07.090
as well is Excel.

00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:08.180
From my experience,

00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:10.950
having worked in consulting
at McKinsey for six years,

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:12.732
and having worked with
lots of clients from lots

00:00:12.732 --> 00:00:16.350
of corporations, many
people really struggle

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:18.200
to work properly in Excel

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:21.930
and especially to format their
worksheets in a proper way.

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:23.950
So this is what we're
going to discuss today.

00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:26.300
I will show you how you
can format your sheets

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:28.340
in a way that they look professional,

00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:30.270
I'll show you a couple of tips and tricks

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:31.470
that I picked over time

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:32.720
how you can quickly

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:34.560
in a nice way make sure

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:37.320
that your sheets look
proper and nice and good.

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:39.250
So welcome to another coffee break here

00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:42.130
on my channel Firm Learning,
my name is Heinrich

00:00:42.130 --> 00:00:45.050
and on my channel, I want to
help you to become successful

00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:46.920
in the first years of your career.

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:48.730
So today we're going to tackle this topic

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:49.730
in the following way.

00:00:49.730 --> 00:00:51.120
First, I will share a couple

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:53.240
of general remarks and observations,

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:56.350
why it is indeed important
to format your sheets

00:00:56.350 --> 00:00:57.330
in a decent way.

00:00:57.330 --> 00:01:00.310
And then I will show you
here on my screen, on the PC,

00:01:00.310 --> 00:01:02.770
some practical tips
what you can actually do

00:01:02.770 --> 00:01:04.850
to format your sheets in a pragmatic way.

00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:05.683
So let's start.

00:01:05.683 --> 00:01:07.790
Why is it even necessary
to format your sheets?

00:01:07.790 --> 00:01:09.440
Well first, it just helps

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:11.230
to bring the key messages across.

00:01:11.230 --> 00:01:13.313
An ill-formated sheet will
be much more difficult

00:01:13.313 --> 00:01:16.580
for yourself or for your
clients to orientate yourself

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:17.413
in the data.

00:01:17.413 --> 00:01:20.520
It is just less accessible,
less intuitive to look at.

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:22.610
But the second reason
is that it just helps

00:01:22.610 --> 00:01:23.660
with the look and feel

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:26.800
with the end product quality
of the sheets that you produce.

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:28.760
And indeed in professional services,

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:31.270
every single document every single sheet

00:01:31.270 --> 00:01:32.950
that you send out to a client

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:36.480
would just be expected to meet
a higher level of quality.

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:39.360
Then maybe would be okay
internally in the client

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:40.710
organization that you work for.

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:42.210
Many clients walk around

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:44.390
with the expectation that what you deliver

00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:47.300
as a BCG consultant, as bank consultant

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:48.960
is just on a higher level

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:51.750
just meets quality
standards, which are higher

00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:54.620
than the standards that they
hold themselves accountable

00:01:54.620 --> 00:01:55.453
for.

00:01:55.453 --> 00:01:57.790
And indeed many consulting
firms have internalized that

00:01:57.790 --> 00:02:00.000
so most project leads, most partners,

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:01.830
will hold you accountable.

00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:04.840
That you indeed deliver
results to the highest levels.

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:06.750
So that being said, let me share a couple

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:07.840
of practical things

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:10.141
what you can do to just
improve the formatting

00:02:10.141 --> 00:02:12.790
improve the look and feel of your sheets.

00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:13.623
So let's just

00:02:13.623 --> 00:02:14.660
look at this random file.

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:16.510
Lets assume now we want to work with it

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:18.910
format it in a proper way
to make it client ready.

00:02:18.910 --> 00:02:20.010
And indeed if you look at this

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:22.260
it looks quite messy for sure

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:24.500
it's not the way how it should be

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:25.780
if you send something like this

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:26.613
to a client.

00:02:26.613 --> 00:02:28.302
And let's just look at
the what we see here.

00:02:28.302 --> 00:02:32.550
So this apparently is a
merchandise from different seasons.

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:34.810
So for instance, from
an apparel company where

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:37.720
the season code, the supplier
code and then the quantity

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:40.010
and the purchase price
that is available here,

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:40.930
so let's get started.

00:02:40.930 --> 00:02:43.330
And the first thing that I
noticed is here that the title

00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:46.520
of this fiLe copy of book
one, this is not great, right.

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:49.480
You should never send a
file with such a title.

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:52.440
So let's look into it and
indeed save it in a proper way.

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:55.380
So usually you go with
something like this so the date,

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:58.680
so it started with the year,
then the month, then the day

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:02.790
and maybe then something
like merchandise overview

00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:06.080
2021 version one, if we
just start doing this.

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:07.120
So this could be one way.

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:09.350
And then also notice here, this one book,

00:03:09.350 --> 00:03:11.320
so this is also always
really something that you

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:13.070
would like to name it a proper way.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:17.340
So for instance, something
like merchandise.

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:18.910
Overview to make it consistent.

00:03:18.910 --> 00:03:21.980
So that's a start, let's
continue by looking into this.

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:24.690
So here you see a mess
of different font sizes,

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:26.891
different font types,
and so on and so forth.

00:03:26.891 --> 00:03:30.530
So again, this doesn't
look the way it should.

00:03:30.530 --> 00:03:34.700
So lets standardize this
make it the same font size,

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:37.450
make it the same font type.

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:40.350
So at least this is now
all in the same style.

00:03:40.350 --> 00:03:42.940
Then one little thing
that I also like to do is

00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:45.330
always to insert a little column here

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:46.983
to the left and then the buff.

00:03:47.930 --> 00:03:50.113
And then we make this
a little bit smaller,

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:53.450
just quite a little bit of distance.

00:03:53.450 --> 00:03:55.411
This just in my experience gives it a nice

00:03:55.411 --> 00:03:56.700
and clean look.

00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:59.670
Here I don't think we need this underline.

00:03:59.670 --> 00:04:01.720
And then here also look at this title

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:04.210
this is like a long, messy title.

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:06.610
So again, one thing
that I often like to do

00:04:06.610 --> 00:04:09.970
is to have a main title
and then the subtitle.

00:04:09.970 --> 00:04:13.770
So let's potentially
call this something like

00:04:13.770 --> 00:04:17.980
merchandise overview as the main title.

00:04:17.980 --> 00:04:19.380
And then we can call it

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:24.380
as a subtitle just to say
for this season 2020 and 2021

00:04:26.170 --> 00:04:27.003
report

00:04:30.361 --> 00:04:31.610
and then we can delete this.

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:34.680
This might potentially be bolded.

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:37.340
Now let's make sure that
here all the columns are of

00:04:37.340 --> 00:04:38.370
proper size

00:04:38.370 --> 00:04:40.130
just space it out something like this.

00:04:40.130 --> 00:04:41.410
Next we want to do the border

00:04:41.410 --> 00:04:45.580
so here what I like to do is
always to give the borders here

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:48.010
to the main data, but not
necessarily to the rest.

00:04:48.010 --> 00:04:48.980
So we do something like this

00:04:48.980 --> 00:04:50.700
and now to make it a bit, look better.

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:52.340
Here you go on view.

00:04:52.340 --> 00:04:54.420
And then you take away the grid lines.

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:57.460
My experience really is if
you take away the grid lines

00:04:57.460 --> 00:04:59.650
it all looks a bit cleaner.

00:04:59.650 --> 00:05:02.010
Now here you go for a dark color

00:05:02.010 --> 00:05:05.220
something like this, dark
blue, and then here, the white

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:08.210
and then you could
potentially also bold this.

00:05:08.210 --> 00:05:09.490
Now I would also like to here

00:05:09.490 --> 00:05:11.300
reduce the distance a little bit.

00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:13.830
Now, next thing we can do is
really to freeze the panes

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:15.650
because now currently, if I'm scrolling

00:05:15.650 --> 00:05:17.720
I'm not seeing the headers anymore.

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:20.230
So you click here view freeze panes

00:05:20.230 --> 00:05:24.800
freeze panes that this, this
thing here above is fixed.

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:28.070
This also makes it look
quite nice, much better.

00:05:28.070 --> 00:05:28.980
So let's make sure

00:05:28.980 --> 00:05:30.830
that all these number
formats are clean as well.

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:32.760
So you see that this is in some weird way

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:33.593
that this is centered

00:05:33.593 --> 00:05:35.210
even though this should
be aligned to the right

00:05:35.210 --> 00:05:38.150
so lets just use here the format painter

00:05:38.150 --> 00:05:41.293
to make sure that this is
always setup in a proper way.

00:05:42.260 --> 00:05:43.890
Now you can see that this is an ID

00:05:43.890 --> 00:05:45.080
so I would be fine with that.

00:05:45.080 --> 00:05:46.850
I would now, continue to form it.

00:05:46.850 --> 00:05:48.180
This though the quantity here

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:52.150
it would always be good to
have some separators, right

00:05:52.150 --> 00:05:53.520
for all the thousand.

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:55.320
So let's do that.

00:05:55.320 --> 00:05:56.153
Now we can see

00:05:56.153 --> 00:05:58.690
that this is done in here for
the purchase price, for sure.

00:05:58.690 --> 00:06:01.570
This is something we would
like to do as well, but here,

00:06:01.570 --> 00:06:03.900
now let's assume we
know that this is Euro,

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:06.870
then it definitely would be
nice to also include this

00:06:06.870 --> 00:06:09.740
in such an accounting format
to have the Euro sign.

00:06:09.740 --> 00:06:12.370
So if we now open the old
one and now compare it

00:06:12.370 --> 00:06:14.500
to the new one, I hope
that you agree that now

00:06:14.500 --> 00:06:17.900
the new one looks nicer and
cleaner to what you had before.

00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:20.120
And indeed this really is quick right now

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:22.960
even with explaining it just
took me a couple of minutes.

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:25.030
And usually of course it's even faster

00:06:25.030 --> 00:06:26.770
if you just do it on your own.

00:06:26.770 --> 00:06:28.060
Just in addition to that

00:06:28.060 --> 00:06:31.240
let's imagine you have a
bit of a more complex layout

00:06:31.240 --> 00:06:34.880
with maybe different types of
tables next to each other's.

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:37.640
And you'd like to do that as
well, but here are two tips.

00:06:37.640 --> 00:06:39.340
So my general recommendation would be

00:06:39.340 --> 00:06:41.790
that if you know start
building different tables,

00:06:41.790 --> 00:06:44.420
rather build them one under the other,

00:06:44.420 --> 00:06:46.120
instead of now adding them here

00:06:46.120 --> 00:06:49.140
to the right, this is just
the standard way of modeling.

00:06:49.140 --> 00:06:51.300
Especially if you have more complex tables

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:53.710
with maybe times here in the columns

00:06:53.710 --> 00:06:56.020
then it's always nicer cleaner to do it

00:06:56.020 --> 00:06:58.400
from top to bottom then from left to right

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:00.410
because then you potentially, for instance

00:07:00.410 --> 00:07:02.780
could also use the same date fields

00:07:02.780 --> 00:07:05.290
then throughout the table
for different lines.

00:07:05.290 --> 00:07:07.650
Again, there might be
some exceptions to that

00:07:07.650 --> 00:07:09.900
especially if you make
dashboards for instance

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:11.800
then it might make sense to
do it in a different way.

00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:14.410
And here indeed, if you would
like to learn more about that

00:07:14.410 --> 00:07:15.840
I once made a distinct video

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:17.230
with a couple of tips on how you can

00:07:17.230 --> 00:07:20.080
build quite powerful and
easy dashboards in excel.

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:21.600
So I will link it somewhere above here

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:23.660
if you would like to
learn more about that.

00:07:23.660 --> 00:07:25.020
But now back to this,
because indeed if here

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:26.920
you would have different types of tables

00:07:26.920 --> 00:07:28.150
one after the other.

00:07:28.150 --> 00:07:29.490
So how would you do that right?

00:07:29.490 --> 00:07:31.130
And let's imagine we might have even more

00:07:31.130 --> 00:07:32.750
so here I'll probably do it.

00:07:32.750 --> 00:07:34.110
Yes, I would do it exactly like this right

00:07:34.110 --> 00:07:36.060
so I just here then copy the title.

00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:38.440
Of course, this might also
be now a different title.

00:07:38.440 --> 00:07:41.230
And then I would leave
like one line distance and

00:07:41.230 --> 00:07:44.320
then probably also make this
distance a little bit shorter.

00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:46.670
And by that now you could
build all kinds of stuff

00:07:46.670 --> 00:07:48.980
one after or one below the other,

00:07:48.980 --> 00:07:51.271
and then keep such a nice clean look

00:07:51.271 --> 00:07:53.100
for all of this to keep going.

00:07:53.100 --> 00:07:53.940
Now let me end

00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:56.110
with an additional little
formatting convention.

00:07:56.110 --> 00:07:58.860
And this is especially
important if you're now working

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:02.060
with more complex files,
more complex models.

00:08:02.060 --> 00:08:04.810
Because indeed, usually it's
important also with colors

00:08:04.810 --> 00:08:06.651
distinguish the input sets

00:08:06.651 --> 00:08:09.220
from the output or the calculated sets.

00:08:09.220 --> 00:08:12.630
So let's imagine now we would
like to calculate the price

00:08:12.630 --> 00:08:16.450
per unit, just here to
make a basic example.

00:08:16.450 --> 00:08:19.380
So now probably what we
would like to do is just

00:08:19.380 --> 00:08:22.710
form it this over here, just
to have it in the same way.

00:08:22.710 --> 00:08:24.618
Now we would calculate that by

00:08:24.618 --> 00:08:28.853
dividing the total purchase
price by the units.

00:08:30.020 --> 00:08:30.990
So just like this.

00:08:30.990 --> 00:08:32.810
Okay. And now what we would love to do is

00:08:32.810 --> 00:08:35.570
as you can can see that this
is now a calculated field

00:08:35.570 --> 00:08:37.550
and now not really an input field

00:08:37.550 --> 00:08:39.310
rather we just input the raw data.

00:08:39.310 --> 00:08:42.820
And here one convention is
to color calculated fields

00:08:42.820 --> 00:08:46.830
in a dark blue, something
like this, for instance.

00:08:46.830 --> 00:08:48.360
So here now on first side

00:08:48.360 --> 00:08:50.720
you see that the black sets are sets

00:08:50.720 --> 00:08:52.970
that you hard copy that you hard input

00:08:52.970 --> 00:08:55.520
and then the blue sets
are calculated ones.

00:08:55.520 --> 00:08:58.240
So again, this just really
helps if you have a more complex

00:08:58.240 --> 00:09:01.110
larger model to really understand
what are all the inputs,

00:09:01.110 --> 00:09:04.250
what are the sets that you
hard coded, hard typed into it,

00:09:04.250 --> 00:09:06.080
what are all the output
sets, which probably

00:09:06.080 --> 00:09:08.229
you should not directly
manipulate, but rather

00:09:08.229 --> 00:09:12.130
they are values derived from
the value of some other sets.

